{"qid":"Q210688","names":["Asuka Japan"],"title":"Asuka Japan","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Asuka period (飛鳥時代, c. 538–710) takes its name from the Asuka region of Yamato Province (modern Nara Prefecture) where successive imperial courts were established, and is defined by the introduction of Buddhism from Baekje in 538, the Taika Reform of 645 that restructured government on Chinese Tang-dynasty models, and the promulgation of the Taihō Code in 701. Prince Shōtoku (574–622), regent for Empress Suiko, promoted Buddhist institutions and issued the Seventeen-Article Constitution (604), articulating Confucian-Buddhist principles of governance. The period ended when Empress Genmei relocated the capital to Nara in 710, inaugurating the Nara period.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Asuka polity's core extent in the Yamato heartland is well attested by Japanese chronicles (Nihon Shoki, Kojiki) and archaeological evidence; projection to outlying regions of Kyushu and northern Honshu relies on less reliable early chronicle accounts.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Asuka period","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asuka_period"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Prince Shōtoku","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_Sh%C5%8Dtoku"}],"britannica":"event/Asuka-period","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Asuka period","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Emperor Kinmei","nativeName":"欽明天皇","roman":"Kinmei-tennō","start":539,"end":571,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Emperor Bidatsu","nativeName":"敏達天皇","roman":"Bidatsu-tennō","start":572,"end":585,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"image